%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Operating Systems for Embedded Systems}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\nsl{Advances in microcontroller technology continue to open up new application fields for embedded systems. In parallel with this trend, the equipment controlled by embedded systems has become more sophisticated, often incorporating many functions in one product. Embedded systems have grown in scale and complexity as a result. Moreover, as products increasingly adopt digital technology, advanced processors have enabled more of the processing to be implemented in software, making embedded systems all the more important.
\newline
This means, in new application the developer is more and more interested to do the software development with the support of an operating system. Especially in large scale systems the effect on reducing development cost with an operating system is important.
\newline
Even in the small-scale embedded system field, the growing scale and complexity of software and the need for fast development turnaround time have made improving software productivity a pressing need. The use of C and other high-level languages have become increasingly common for this reason.
\newline
Real time operating systems fulfil all the requirements from the developer. They guarantee a response of the system in specific time. The development cycle becomes much shorter if the user could do the development on the target. A cross-development with the download the executable program in the target, test it, change and do the same procedure one's more for the next error is not an optimal development cycle. Real time operating systems offer a good support for the debugging part.
\newline
There exist only a few drawbacks if you are looking for appropriate real time operating system. The first most important disadvantages of the most real time operating systems are the costs. The second one is that real time operating systems needs a virtual memory system which in the most microcontrollers is not available.
\newline
The virtual memory system itself is really suitable for general purpose applications like a desktop PC. But for embedded systems a virtual memory system makes the application more difficult. In such systems really often an access to the hardware is necessary (control interfaces). Theses programming aspects could only be done in the so called ``kernel-mode'' with the result that such programs more dangerous (collapse of the operating system) and more difficult to test.
\newline
In this chapter we will discuss some aspects of operating systems and than come to the uCLinux, an operating system for microcontroller with and without a virtual memory system.
}
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite a lot of embedded systems runs without a operating system
	\bi 
		\ite only  small algorithmn (e.g. control the window open/close system in a car)
		\ite hard restrictions in the size of memory especially by 8- and 16-bit microcontrollers
		\ite costs of an operating system 
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite reasons for the use of an operating system
	\bi 
		\ite generally the same reasons why we need one for a traditional computer
		\ite but in the case of embedded systems not all services of an operating systems are needed for any device
		\ite embedded systems have a large variety of requirements and environments
		\bi 
			\ite critical applications with high functionality (medical
applications, aircraft systems ..)
			\ite critical applications with small functionality (ABS, cardiac pacemaker ..)
			\ite not very critical applications with varying functionality (mobile phone microwave ofen, washing machine ..)
		\ei
 
	\ei
\ei

\begin{description}
\item[{\rot\bf Special operating system (OS) for embedded systems}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite a standard OS (like Windows or Linux) isn't suitable because
	\bi 
		\ite monolithic kernel has too much feature which not necessary in a specific application
		\ite monolithic kernel is not modular, fault-tolerant, configurable, modifiable
		\ite needs too much memory space (virtual memory)
		\ite not designed for mission-critical applications	
	\ei
\ei
\bigskip
\item[{\rot\bf Key features of an OS for embedded systems }]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite configurability \pr no single OS will fit all needs, so configurability needed
	\bi 
		\ite simplest form \pr remove unused functions (by linker ?)
		\ite conditional compilation (using \#if and \#ifdef commands)
		\ite dynamic data might be replaced by static data
		\ite remove unnecessary functions or moduls by a kernel programming (Linux, eCos, VxWORKS)
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite handling of device drivers
	\bi 
		\ite access to interfaces 
		\ite device drivers handled by tasks instead of integrated drivers
		\ite improve predictability; everything goes through scheduler
  		\ite in embedded systems mostly a lot of different interfaces available, so no every device needs the support by all versions of the OS
	\ei
\ei
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_1_RTOS_StandardOS,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.1 Integration of Devices Drivers in an OS
\end{center}

\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite handling of interupts
	\bi 
		\ite for standard OS: serious source of unreliability
		\ite it should be possible to let interrupts directly start or stop tasks (by storing the
tasks start address in the interrupt table)
	\ei
\ei

\item[{\rot\bf Available OS for embedded systems}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite QNX
	\bi 
		\ite real time operating system based on micro core technology
		\ite suitable for small and large embedded systems
		\ite tasks are separated in its one address space
		\ite scheduling event driven (restrictions in time based scheduling) 
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite VxWORKS
	\bi 
		\ite small VxWorks Kernel
		\ite no virtual memory space (dangerous if a task is not cooperative)
		\ite scheduling time, priority and event driven
		\ite implemented on a lot of microcontroller
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite OSEK/VDX
	\bi
		\ite scalable real time OS build for application in the automotive area
		\ite event driven execution of tasks
		\ite operates with fixed priorities 
		\ite tasks are grouped in basic tasks and extenden tasks
		\ite no virtual memory system 
	\ei 
	\os{\newpage}
	\ite eCos
	\bi
		\ite open source system
		\ite scalable system (the configuration allows the application writer to impose their requirements on the run-time component)
		\ite OS and application software are specific for an embedded system
		\ite scheduling are time and event driven
		\ite no virtual memory system 
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite uCLinux
	\bi
		\ite open source system
		\ite scalable and portable (especially for 32 bit microcontrollers)
		\ite run with a non virtual and a virtual memory system
		\ite scheduling is time and priority driven
		\ite with the RTAI (\underline{r}eal \underline{t}ime \underline{a}pplication \underline{i}nterface) also a event driven scheduling mechanismn possible
		\ite for small and large embedded systems
	\ei
\ei 
\end{description}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Functions of uCLinux}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\bi 
	\ite the general idea of an ``embedded Linux'' is to store an image (Kernel plus necessary moduls) in a non-volatile memory (Flash-EEPROM, SD-Card)
	\ite after a reset (or power on reset) the image ist copied in the RAM and executed
	\ite the size of the copied image are also noun as a ``footprint''
	\ite the additional feature of uCLinux (Linux for microcontroller) is that it works without a virtual memory system (VM)
	\bi 
		\ite suitable for processor without MMU
		\ite low memory footprint
		\ite according to the processors (Motorola/Freescale Coldfire) low power consumption applications are possible
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite with the CFV4e (MMU is available) uCLinux could run with a virtual memory system or with a non virtual memory system 
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{description}
\item[{\rot\bf Boot sequence}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite the boot procedure of uCLinux differs from a standard boot procedure
	\ite this procedure offers two advantages
	\bi 
		\ite the development process of building a Linux system is nearly the same as in a standard Linux (the same tools are available)
		\ite the execution time of programs which runs in a RAM is much more faster than in a Flash-EPROM)
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite boot procedure starts immediately after the reset (an interaction with the user isn't necessary)
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_2_Boot,width=0.45\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.2 Boot Procedure
\end{center}

\item[{\rot\bf Scalable behaviour of uCLinux}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi 
	\ite building of a new image (binaries of uCLinux systems) start with a configuration part
	\ite first part is the selection of the ``vendor'' 
	\ite specification of hardware details of the development kit (RAM addresses, ROM addresses)
	\ite the next step is the configuration of necessary moduls and drivers

\ei

\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_3_Vendor,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.3 Specification of Hardware Details
\end{center}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_4_Drivers,width=0.95\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.4 Configuration through a Selection of Moduls or Drivers
\end{center}

\item[{\rot\bf Build procedure}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi 
	\ite after the configuration (make menuconfig)
	\bi 
		\ite start the build process with ``make dep''
		\ite  and ``make''
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite the Kernel-compilation need a special compiler (gcc-version for the CFV4e) und a special binutils (as, ld objtools for the CFV42) (see next chapter)
	\ite the result of of the Kernel-compilation is a image which is in two version available
	\bi 
		\ite file ``linux.bin''
		\bi 
			\ite a loadable image
			\ite could be downloaded with a searial interface an the monitor program ``DBG''
			\ite execute (start the cCLinux) with a ``go''-command of the debugger (e.g, go 0x20000)
			\ite useful for test of a configuration
		\ei
		\bigskip
		\ite file ``romfs''
		\bi 
  			\ite a image which could be loaded in the FlashEPROM
			\ite need a ``Flash''-load program like CF-Flasher
			\ite cCLinux started after the next reset or power-on-reset with the mentioned boot sequence
		\ei
	\ei
\ei

\item[{\rot\bf Working with uCLinux in the target}]~\\[-3ex]

\bi 
	\ite establish a serial communication link between the target and the PC
	\bi 
		\ite works as standard input/output (stdio) for messages from uCLinux
		\ite on the Pc-side a terminal program like ``minicom'' (Linux`` or ''Hyperterminal`` (Windows'' is useful
	\ei
\ei
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_5_Minicom,width=0.95\textwidth}\\
\end{center}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_5_Minicom_P,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.5 Configuration of Minicom
\end{center}

\bi 
	\ite if an ethernet link are established
	\bi 
		\ite mount PC partition by NFS
		\ite execute a program which are located on the PC in the target (run the CFVe4- instructions)
	\ei
\ei
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_6_uCLinux,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.6 Standard Input/Output of uCLinux
\end{center}
\os{\newpage}
\item[{\rot\bf Restrictions when using non VM-systems}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite CFV4e controllers could operate with a VM (virtual memory) an a non-VM-System
	\ite in the case of working witout a VM-System
	\bi 
		\ite a userprogram has the ful access about the whole memory space
		\ite as a result no segmentation fault is possible
		\ite no dynamic heap \pr instead uCLinux use a global memory pool (kernel
               free memory pool)
		\ite no obvious implementation of malloc due to lack of heap
		\ite no fork sytem call \pr an implementation of ``copy on write'' strategy is n't possible (An alternative is the old BSD vfork and all his limits.)
	\ei
\ei
\end{description}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Cross Compilation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\bi 
	\ite cross compilation is typical for programming in embedded systems
	\ite write, link and simulate programs for an embedded system on a PC (for example)
	\ite the executable programs than habe to be downloaded and started on the target under the control of a monitor program (mostly with a serial connection between the PC and the target)
	\ite after the test in the target the executable program could be stored in a EPROM and ececuted without an monitor program
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_7_CrossCompilation,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.7 Cross Compilation
\end{center} 

\os{\newpage}
\begin{description}
\item [{\rot\bf Cross compilation with uCLinux}]~\\[-3ex]
\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite in uCLinux systems the cross compilation could done for the same reasons as in standard systems
	\ite the advantage in this case is that the same debugging tools are available for the test on the PC and for the test in the target system (remote debugging)
	\bigskip
	\ite to compile a program for the CFV4e controller a specific toolchain is necessary (the standard C-compiler and the standard binutils don't support the features of the CFV4e)
	\ite the same toolchain is also used to build the uCLinux 
	\ite to generate all parts of the toolchain the standard C-compiler and C-libraries are involved
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
 \begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
Tool & description\\
\hline
m68k-elf-gcc & C-Compiler for CFV4e Controller\\
\hline
m68k-elf-as & Assembler for CFV42 Controller\\
\hline
m68-elf-ld & Linker for CFV42 Controller\\
\hline
\hline
m68-elf-ar & Archiver, tool to build, extract and show the contains \\
 & of libraries \\
\hline 
m68k-elf-nm & extracts the symbols of an ececutable file\\
\hline
m68k-elf-objdump & extract the contains of an executable file \\
 & (e.g. disassemble files)\\
\hline
m68k-elf-objcopy & tool to generate loadable files from \\
 & the output from the linker (e.g. ``x.bin'' or ``x.S19'') \\
\hline
 \end{tabular}
\end{center}
\os{\newpage}
\item [{\rot\bf Executable files}]~\\[-3ex]	
\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite standard output of the linker (``x.elf'') isn't downloadable
	\bi 
		\ite this file needs a VM-System for a load and an execute
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite instead ``x.elf'' the tool m68k-elf-objcopy generates a ``x.bin'' and/or ``x.S19''-file
	\bi 
		\ite both files are directly downloadable and don't need a VM-System for execution
		\ite this means both file are linked on absolut addresses
		\ite the ``x.bin''-format is a binary format (could not be read with an editor)
		\ite the ``x.S19''.format is a text format which is readable with a standard editor
	\ei
\ei
\os{\newpage}
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_8_Info_ELF,width=0.9\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.8 Information in a ``x.elf''-file
\end{center} 
\begin{center}
\epsfig{file=figures/Fig_6_9_Info_S19,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
Fig 6.9 File in ``x.S19''-Format
\end{center}

\item [{\rot\bf C-Libraries in cross-compilation}]~\\[-3ex]	
\bigskip
\bi 
	\ite for CFV4e most of the standard C-Libraries are available 
	\ite sometimes exist a special version of a C-Library
	\bi 
		\ite e.g. the library uClibc instead of glibc
		\ite uClibc is a smaller library compared with glibc
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite the standard input/output (functions printf, scanf ..) not supported
	\ite instead of standard input/output appropriate monitor function used
	\ite in the case of ``dbug'' the TRAP 15 - functions could be used
\ei
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{l l l}
Trap-Nr. & Function & Parameter \\
\hline
\#15 - 13 & Out\_char & D0 = 0x13, D1 = character \\
\#15 - 10 & In\_char & D0 = 0x10, D1 = character \\
\#15 - 14 & Char\_present & D0 = 0x14, \\
 & & D0 = 0 \pr no character \\
 & & D0 != 0 \pr character available \\
\#15 - 0 & Exit\_to\_dbug & D0 = 0,\\ 	
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{description}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{User Programming in uCLinux}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	
\bi 
	\ite user programming means that a program runs in the user mode of the CFV4e 
	\ite if the VM-System is activated \pr the user don't have an access to an absolut memory address
	\bi 
		\ite especially a direct access to the registers of interfaces isn't allowed
	\ei
	\bigskip
	\ite the restrictions of the user mode from the CFV4e means also that an access to SR-register, VBR and so one are not allowed
	\ite these access could only be done in the supervisor mode (kernel program)
\ei
\bigskip
\bi
	\ite development of program should be done in tree steps 
	\ite first try to generate a program with a cross-compilation for the target, load it and test it
	\ite second merge the program together with an image of the operating system and load it
	\ite third if necessary implement a autoexec function in the init process of uCLinux
\ei

\begin{description}
\item[{\rot\bf Cross-compilation with uCLinux}]~\\
\bi 
	\ite the cross-compilation with uCLinux is the same procedure as the standard cross-compilation for CFV4e processors
	\ite in preparation for the next two steps it is helpful to use a separat directory which hold all the necessary files (C-files, Header-files, makefile)
	\ite put this directory in the uCLinux-file-system /uCLinux/user/
	\ite after the compilation mount the directory with NFS in the uCLinux form the target and execute and test the program
\ei
\bigskip
\item[{\rot\bf Download together with an image of uCLinux}]~\\
\bi
	\ite the second step is to integrate the program in an image of uCLinux
	\ite additional to the cross-compilation the program have to be integrated in the build procedure of uCLinux
	\bi 
		\ite the ``central''-makefile for building all the user applications \\ (/uCLinux/user/makefile) should expanded with an instruction like
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l l l}
		    DIRSc\$(CONFIG\_USER\_CAN)& += & can4linux & (present line)\\
		    DIRSc\$(CONFIG\_USER\_PROGRAM)& += & program & (new line)\\
		   \end{tabular}
		\ite to make the program visible in configuration tool (make menuconfig) of uCLinux the file config.in (/uCLinux/user/config.in) should expand by
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l l}
		    bool 'can4linux' & CONFIG\_USER\_CAN & (present line) \\
		    bool 'program' & CONFIG\_USER\_PROGRAM & (new line) \\
		   \end{tabular}
		\ite to give some more or less information about the program the somebody how will do the configuration the file Configure.help \\
		(/uCLinux/user/Documentation/Configure.help) should expand by
		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
		   CONFIG\_USER\_CAN& \\
		    & This package contains some examples\\
		    & using can4linux device driver.\\
		    & \\
		   CONFIG\_USER\_PROGRAM& \\
		    & This programm adds my first functions \\
		    & to uCLinux. I'm not sure whether \\
		    & somebody can use these functions\\
		   \end{tabular}
		\os{\newpage}
		\ite if in the configuration step the program is selected, the resulting image contains the program
		\ite to test the image (uCLinux) and the program one's more it is downloadable with the monitor ``dbug''
		\ite the downloaded uCLinux (including the program) could be startet with the instruction ``go 0x20000'' from the monitor
	\ei
\os{\newpage}
\ei
\item[{\rot\bf Autostart of a program with uCLinux}]~\\
\bi 
	\ite if the program (which was downloaded together with the uCLinux-image) should used, the user have to start it
	\ite uCLinux offers an automatic start of programs (function of the program init which execute the sript file /etc/inittab)
	\ite the sript-file inittab ask an another skript-file rcS \\ (placed in /etc/rc.d/rcS) and this file switch over to rc.local (/etc/rc.d/rc.local)
	\ite an auto start could be done by an addition in the file rc.local (but this means that the user have to change this file and this is obviously not an autostart, escpecially not for the first time)
	\ite to do a real auto start the file \\
	/uCLinux/defconfigs/vendors/senTec/etc/rc.d/rc.local have to changed an than a new image habe to be created
	\ite when this image is loaded in the FlushEPROM (with the program CFFlusher) then after a reset the program is automatical started witout an interaction with the user
\ei

\end{description}